        "Node name for S&R": "MarkdownNote"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "## MiniMax H3 — FL2VA + Sol-Attn (RTX 3090)\n\n**FL2VA** = first + last frame -> video **with native stereo audio**, generated jointly in one pass.\nBoth `LoadImage` nodes are required for true FL2VA; disconnect `last_frame` for plain i2v, or both for t2va.\n\n### Model chain\n`UNETLoader` -> `MiniMax H3 Sigma Shift` -> **`Sol-Attn MiniMax H3`** -> scheduler + guider\n\nSol-Attn must sit on the MODEL path feeding **both** `BasicScheduler` and `BasicGuider`.\n\n### Note on guidance\nH3 is **guidance-free** — `BasicGuider` takes positive conditioning only. There is no negative prompt and no CFG.\n\n### Resolution / length\n`864 x 480`, `length 124` (~5s @ 24fps) is the 3090-friendly default: roughly **13k tokens**, comfortably above the 8192 `min_seq_len` gate so Sol-Attn engages.\n`length` snaps up to the model's 17k+5 grid. 720p (`1344 x 768`) works but is much slower and near the VRAM ceiling with the 21 GB INT8 transformer.\n\n### ⚠ width and height MUST be multiples of 32\nThe VAE downsamples 16x, then the DiT patchifies 2x2 — so a multiple of **16 is not enough**.\nA size like `688` (= 16 x 43, odd latent) crashes with:\n\n`RuntimeError: shape '[1, 24, 1, 1, 21, 2, 15, 2]' is invalid for input of size 30960`\n\nThis only happens with **keyframes connected**: `model.py:508` pads the main video latent to patch size, but `_cond_video_rows` (`model.py:477`) does **not** pad the keyframe latent. t2va at the same size runs fine.\n\nNote the `step=32` on the widgets only constrains the spinner arrows — typed values, and anything **linked in** from a resolution selector or `GetImageSize`, bypass it.\n\n| megapixels | 16:9 output |\n|---|---|\n| 0.2 | 608 x 352 |\n| 0.4 | 864 x 480 |\n| 0.6 | 1056 x 608 |\n| 0.98 | 1344 x 768 |\n\nPortrait equivalents: `480 x 672`, `480 x 704`, `608 x 1088`.\n"
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
    {
      "id": 21,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        -1180,
